<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Euclid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 300 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Euclid was a Greek mathematician of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, active around 300 BCE, whose Elements became the foundational textbook of geometry. His work was translated into Arabic during the translation movement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, preserving and transmitting it to later Islamic and European scholars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/euclid.jpg" alt="Euclid" width="311" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jusepe de Ribera (Spanish / Italian, 1591 - 1652) (1591 - 1652) – artist (Spanish / Italian) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italian_-_Euclid_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The House of Wisdom (Bayt al-Hikma), traditionally associated with Harun al-Rashid and reaching its zenith under al-Ma'mun, sponsored the systematic translation of Greek, Persian, Indian and Syriac texts into Arabic, a project that preserved Aristotle, <b>Euclid</b>, Galen and Claudius Ptolemy for the medieval world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexandria became one of the greatest cities of the ancient world, home to the Library of Alexandria and the Mouseion, which attracted scholars such as <b>Euclid</b>, Eratosthenes and Herophilus, and to the Pharos lighthouse, one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek literature and thought form one of the foundations of Western culture, and their influence reached the Islamic world, whose scholars translated Aristotle, Galen and <b>Euclid</b> into Arabic in the 8th to 10th centuries.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Humanists printed Archimedes, <b>Euclid</b> and Ptolemy in Greek, and the recovery of rival ancient schools, such as the atomism of Lucretius, showed that the ancients had disagreed among themselves; voyagers found lands, peoples, plants and animals unknown to the ancients; and printing put accurate drawings, maps and tables…</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The court also kept scholars and poets: Ptolemy II paid stipends to Callimachus and Theocritus, the mathematician <b>Euclid</b> worked under his patronage, and the chief librarian of the Library of Alexandria was also the crown prince’s tutor.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ptolemaic-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ptolemaic Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>An electric traffic signal with red and green lights was installed at <b>Euclid</b> Avenue and East 105th Street in Cleveland, Ohio.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-5">5 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
